 cite27†4.2.1 Arithmetic Optimization Evaluation L38:       2. cite28†4.2.2 MSM – Dataflow Optimization Evaluation L39:       3. cite29†4.2.3 NTT – Dataflow Optimization Evaluation L40:     3. cite30†4.3 MORPH vs. SotAs L41:       1. cite31†4.3.1 NTT L42:       2. cite32†4.3.2 MSM L43:     4. cite33†4.4 Ablation Study - Batch Size L44:   6. cite34†5 Conclusion L45:     1. cite35†5.1 Acknowledgments L46:   7. cite36†References L47: cite37†License: CC BY 4.0†info.arxiv.org L48: \nL49: arXiv:2604.17808v1 [cs.AR] 20 Apr 2026\nL50: # Enabling AI ASICs for Zero Knowledge Proof\nL51: \nL52: Conference: 63rd ACM/IEEE Design Automation Conference; July 26–29, 2026; Long Beach, CA, USA 63rd ACM/IEEE Design Automation Conference (DAC ’26), July 26–29, 2026, Long Beach, CA, USA DOI: cite38†10.1145/3770743.3804402†doi.org ISBN: 979-8-4007-2254-7/2026/07\nL53: Jianming Tong Note: Both authors contributed equally to this research.\nL54: email: jianming.tong@gatech.edu Affiliation: Georgia Institute of Technology, Atlanta, Georgia, USA, 30332 , Jingtian Dang email: dangjingtian@gatech.edu Affiliation: Georgia Institute of Technology, Atlanta, Georgia, USA , Simon Langowski email: slangowski@mit.edu Affiliation: Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Cambridge, Massachusetts, USA , Tianhao Huang email: tianhaoh@mit.edu Affiliation: Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Cambridge, Massachusetts, USA , Asra Ali email: asraa@google.com Affiliation: Google, Austin, Texas, USA , Jeremy Kun email: jkun@google.com Affiliation: Google, Portland, Oregon, USA , Jevin Jiang email: jevinjiang@google.com Affiliation: Google, Sunnyvale, California, USA , Srinivas Devadas email: devadas@mit.edu Affiliation: Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Cambridge, Massachusetts, USA and Tushar Krishna email: tushar@ece.gatech.edu Affiliation: Georgia Institute of Technology, Atlanta, Georgia, USA, 30332\nL55: © cc\nL56: ###### Abstract.\nL57: Zero-knowledge proof (ZKP) provers remain costly because multi-scalar multiplication (MSM) and number-theoretic transforms (NTTs) dominate runtime as they need significant computation. AI ASICs such as TPUs provide massive matrix throughput and SotA energy efficiency. We present MORPH, the first framework that reformulates ZKP kernels to match AI-ASIC execution.\nL58: We introduce Big-$T$ complexity, a hardware-aware complexity model that exposes heterogeneous bottlenecks and layout-transformation costs ignored by Big-$O$.\nL59: Guided by this analysis, (1) at arithmetic level, MORPH develops an MXU-centric extended-RNS lazy reduction that converts high-precision modular arithmetic into dense low-precision GEMMs, eliminating all carry chains, and (2) at dataflow level, MORPH constructs a unified-sharding layout-stationary TPU Pippenger MSM and optimized 3/5-step NTT that avoid on-TPU shuffles to minimize costly memory reorganization.\nL60: Implemented in JAX, MORPH enables TPUv6e8 to achieve up-to 10$\\times$ higher throughput on NTT and comparable throughput on MSM than GZKP. Our code: cite39†https://github.com/EfficientPPML/MORPH†github.com .\nL61: ^{†}^{†}cc-license: by\nL62: ## 1. Introduction\nL63: Zero-knowledge proofs (ZKPs) have evolved from a theoretical construct into a core building block for practical verifiable computation and scalable blockchains. They allow services to outsource computation while keeping data private and verification cheap, but the prover remains prohibitively expensive, e.g., generating a proof for ImageNet ViT requires nearly one hour (cite40†Zhang et al., 2025 ).\nL64: Across widely deployed protocols (cite41†Groth, 2016 ; cite42†Gabizon et al., 2019 ), prover runtime is dominated by multi-scalar multiplication (MSM) and the number-theoretic transform (NTT), which commonly account for $\\approx$70% and 20–30% of total latency, respectively (cite43†Ji et al., 2024 ; cite44†Zhang et al., 2021 ).\nL65: cite45†Image: Refer to caption Figure 1. MORPH’s deployment flow with dataflow and arithmetic optimizations to accelerate ZKP on TPU.\nL66: Both MSM and NTT expose abundant parallelism at practical ZKP sizes, making parallel hardware promising for acceleration. Among these platforms, AI ASICs (Google TPU (cite46†Jouppi et al., 2023 ), AWS Trainium (cite47†Tra, 2025 ) etc.) offer extreme compute density and energy efficiency, significantly outperforming general-purpose accelerators at scale (cite48†Tong et al., 2026a ).\nL67: Architecturally, a TPU contains giant heterogeneous cores, including a matrix multiplication engine (MXU) and a vectorized processing engine (VPU). Each MXU/VPU contains orders of magnitude (1024/16$\\times$) more multiply-accumulates (MACs) than one corresponding core in a GPU (cite49†Austin et al., 2025 ). All MACs in a MXU/VPU execute instruction in the lock-step (SIMD) to substantially reduce per-operation control overhead and improve energy efficiency.\nL68: This paper studies how to systematically repurpose AI ASICs, particularly Google’s TPUs (cite46†Jouppi et al., 2023 ), for ZKP kernels (MSM/NTT) and achieve higher energy efficiency and SoTA throughput at scale.\nL69: Existing ZKP acceleration spans GPUs (cite50†Ma et al., 2023a ; cite43†Ji et al., 2024 ; cite51†Dai and Sunar, 2015 ; cite52†Ma et al., 2023b ), FPGAs (cite53†Aasaraai et al., 2022 ; cite54†Liu et al., 2023 ; cite55†Ray et al., 2023 ; cite56†Pottier et al., 2025 ; cite57†Ohno et al., 2025 ; cite58†Reagen et al., 2021 ) and ASICs (cite59†Samardzic et al., 2024 ; cite44†Zhang et al., 2021 ; cite60†Wang and Gao, 2025 ).\nL70: While these systems hand-craft MSM/NTT algorithms for their respective platforms, they provide no principled way to quantify how far an MSM/NTT algorithm is from the platform’s performance limits with detailed reasoning. Some works provide Big-O complexity (cite61†Kim et al., 2023 ), yet we find a surprising phenomenon: the fastest GPU/TPU algorithms intentionally use the algorithm with higher Big-O to expose massive parallelism and thus achieve higher throughput (cite48†Tong et al., 2026a ).\nL71: Big-O alone is insufficient for modern heterogeneous parallel hardware.\nL72: Furthermore, Big-O cannot capture layout transformation cost, which becomes the dominant bottleneck on AI ASICs. When we port the SotA MSM and NTT algorithms for GPU/FPGA/ZKP-ASIC to TPUs with similar overall throughput, they slow down by 30$\\times$ in our evaluation. This is because a TPU only operates on coarser-grained contiguous data (a 4KB vector register, VReg), and fine-grained data gathering in SotA MSM/NTT algorithms requires expensive shuffles and transposes to move data into contiguous VRegs.\nL73: These layout-induced stalls are invisible under the traditional Big-O model. To address this gap, we introduce Big-$T$ notation, a platform-aware complexity model that measures the sequential bottleneck across heterogeneous pipelined compute units and the latency of layout transformation, providing an asymptotic lower bound for parallel execution on AI ASICs. Big-$T$ exposes two structural inefficiencies in SotA MSM/NTT algorithms on TPUs.\nL74: Arithmetic-level Challenge: ZKP requires modular arithmetic over large prime fields (e.g., 256/377/753 bits), but hardware natively supports much lower precision (e.g., 8/32-bit). Because prime fields lack a natural RNS factorization, the SotA algorithm (cite52†Ma et al., 2023b ; cite62†yrr, 2025 ) performs high-precision arithmetic via digit decomposition and a chain of carry propagation, achieving <1% compute utilization on TPUs.\nL75: Dataflow-level Challenge: The SotA dataflows incur prohibitive communication and storage overheads. In presorted Pippenger MSM (cite43†Ji et al., 2024 ; cite52†Ma et al., 2023b ), sorting is distributed across many GPU CUDA cores. Directly porting this strategy to TPU introduces prohibitive inter-TPU communication. Layout-invariant 3-step NTT (cite48†Tong et al., 2026a ) requires parameters that scale linearly with problem size, resulting in out-of-memory (OOM) failures at large scale.\nL76: In resolving these inefficiencies, we propose MORPH^{1}^{1} 1 MORPH: M atrix-O riented R eformulation of ZKP for P arallel H eterogeneous AI ASICs, the first framework enabling TPU as ZKP accelerator with arithmetic and dataflow level optimizations, as shown in Fig. cite63†1 .\nL77: Arithmetic-level Solution: Since a prime field cannot be represented in a Residue Number System (RNS), MORPH adopts (cite64†Jacquemin et al., 2022 ; cite65†Langowski and Devadas, 2025 ) to encapsulate modular multiplication in a prime field in a larger non-prime finite field that has a RNS representation. This enables high-precision multiplication to be expressed as independent 32-bit vector multiplications with no carry propagation.\nL78: Then, MORPH reforms the modular reduction (RNS reconstruction → modular reduction → RNS decomposition) as low-precision matrix multiplication and parallel vectorized operations to be accelerated by TPU’s MXU and VPU, achieving up-to 90$\\times$ speedup.\nL79: Dataflow-level Solution: To tackle communication and layout reorganization, MORPH proposed LS-PPG to schedule communication-free workload dimensions across devices to avoid inter-TPU communication, ensures layout invariant within individual TPU. To mitigate parameters storage overflow, MORPH’s 5-step NTT recursively partitions workloads to reduce parameter size while preserving the same computational complexity.\nL80: Together, MORPH enables TPUv6e-8 to achieve up-to 10$\\times$ NTT throughput and 1.2$\\times$ MSM throughput than GZKP (cite52†Ma et al., 2023b ) (NVIDIA V100). MORPH makes TPU the SotA throughput machine for high-precision NTT, a promising platform for ZKP acceleration. Our contributions are:\nL81: \nL82:   * $\\bullet$\nL83: BIG-$T$ Complexity: A hardware-aware complexity metric that captures heterogeneous compute spans and layout transformation, enabling principled analysis of MSM/NTT designs on AI ASICs and guiding the construction of layout-stationary pippenger for MSM and 5-step NTT, giving 5.8$\\times$ and 1.6$\\times$ speedup.\nL84: \nL85:   * $\\bullet$\nL86: MXU-centric RNS Lazy Reduction: A matrix-oriented modular reduction for big integer multiplication in prime fields, which replaces sequential carry-propagation arithmetic with MXU-accelerated RNS-lazy reduction, achieving up-to $90\\times$ speedup over the SotA radix decomposed Montgomery reduction.\nL87: \nL88:   * $\\bullet$\nL89: LS-PPG and 5-step Layout Invariant NTT: Both avoid inter-TPU communication and minimize intra-TPU layout reorganization, delivering up-to 5.8$\\times$ speedup over the SoTA MSM algorithm(cite43†Ji et al., 2024 ) on TPU, and reduce parameters storage at scale.\nL90: ## 2. Background and Related Work\nL91: \nL92:  | S huffle  | T ranspose  | MXU  | VPU\nL93: Max Parallelism  | $PAR_{\\text{S}}=4096$  | $PAR_{\\text{T}}=4096$  | $PAR_{\\text{MXU}}=131072$  | $PAR_{\\text{VPU}}=2048$\nL94: Datatype  | u32  | u32  | bf16/i8  | u32\nL95: Requirement  | (8, 128)  | (8, 128)  | (8$\\times$128$\\times$128 MatMul)  | 2048\nL96: Figure 2. TPU programming model. All compute units operate on VRegs with $(8\\times 128)$ 32-bit registers each. Performance hinges on data being laid out such that tiles needed for computation can be loaded directly from VRegs. Values from (cite46†Jouppi et al., 2023 ).\nL97: ### 2.1. Tensor Processing Unit (TPU)\nL98: The TPU (cite46†Jouppi et al., 2023 ) is Google’s AI ASIC designed to accelerate machine learning training and inference with high energy efficiency. All on-chip computation is organized around the Vector Register (VReg) abstraction. Each VReg consists of 8$\\times$128 of 32-bit values executed in lock-step SIMD. All compute units on a TPU, including the VPU, MXU, and XLU, consume and produce data strictly at VReg granularity, as shown in Fig. cite66†2 .\nL99: This uniform but coarse layout enables high efficiency but restricts flexibility: workloads whose shapes do not align with the fixed $(8,128)$ structure incur layout transformation because only full VRegs can be processed each cycle.\nL100: $\\bullet$ Vector Processing Unit (VPU). The VPU performs 32-bit SIMD arithmetic on VRegs. E.g., TPUv4 contains a dual-issue ALU to operate two VRegs concurrently, yielding a peak parallelism of $P_{\\text{VPU}}=2048$ 32-bit operations per cycle. Vectors with length not a multiple of (8, 128) cannot fully utilize VPU’s available parallelism.\nL101: $\\bullet$ Matrix Multiplication Unit (MXU). The MXU in TPUv4 is a large systolic array (128$\\times$128) for int8 and bf16 matrix multiplication. It preloads weight tiles from VRegs and streams activation tiles from VRegs. When normalized to the same datatype, the MXU achieves roughly 16$\\times$ higher peak throughput than the VPU. Full efficiency requires workloads to conform to the MXU’s minimum effective tile of 8$\\times$128$\\times$128.\nL102: Larger gives more weights reuse while smaller or non-aligned matrix shapes leave MXU under-utilized.\nL103: $\\bullet$ Layout Transformation Unit (XLU). XLU performs shuffles, transposes, broadcasts, and reductions of VRegs. XLU is efficient for transformations at VRegs granularity, but costly for element-wise permutations. Shuffling $N$ individual elements may cost $N$ cycles.\nL104: $\\bullet$ HBM and the host CPU serve as the off-chip data sources, but their bandwidth is typically an order of magnitude lower than on-chip VReg bandwidth. Consequently, high-performance TPU kernels favor on-chip data reuse and layout transformation.\nL105: ### 2.2. Performance Metric and Analysis\nL106: TPU’s heterogeneous compute units, each with different parallelism and tiling constraints, make classical models such as Big $O$ and the work–span model inadequate. These models measure total computational work or critical-path latency under the assumption of a homogeneous sequential machine like a CPU, and therefore cannot capture VReg granularity, systolic-array tiling, or the cost of on-chip layout transformations.\nL107: Further, Roofline analysis(cite67†Williams et al., 2009 ) and trace-based profiling quantify how far execution is from hardware limits, but they still do not explain why performance is low.\nL108: ### 2.3. Zero-knowledge Proof (ZKP) Acceleration\nL109: #### 2.3.1. Background on Kernel and Arithmetic in ZKP\nL110: \nL111: Multi-Scalar Multiplication. MSM (cite41†Groth, 2016 ) computes $\\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}S_{n}\\circledast P_{n}$ where each scalar $S_{n}$ is $S^{BL}$ bits and each point $P_{n}$ lies on an elliptic curve that supports point addition (PADD, $\\oplus$). A scalar–point multiplication ($\\circledast$) is realized as repeated PADDs for $S_{n}$ times.\nL112: Number Theory Transformation. NTT (cite41†Groth, 2016 ) converts a polynomial of degree $N$ into its frequency-domain representation by evaluating it at $N$ distinct roots of unity in a finite field. Given an input vector $\\mathbf{x}$ of degree $N$, NTT computes $\\textbf{X}_{k}=\\sum_{n=0}^{N-1}x_{n}\\times\\omega_{N}^{kn}\\bmod\\color[rgb]{0,0.2969,0.6016}{M}$, $k\\in[0,N)$, where $\\omega_{N}$ is a primitive $N$-th root of unity.\nL113: Large prime field. Modern ZKP systems (cite41†Groth, 2016 ) rely on arithmetic over a finite field $\\color[rgb]{0,0.2969,0.6016}{\\mathbb{F}_{M}}$ with a large prime modulus $\\color[rgb]{0,0.2969,0.6016}{M}$ ($256/377/753$ bits). In both elliptic-curve MSM and polynomial-commitment NTT, all additions, multiplications are executed modulo large prime $\\color[rgb]{0,0.2969,0.6016}{M}$.\nL114: #### 2.3.2. Related Work: Arithmetic Implementations\nL115: ZKPs operate over large prime fields $\\color[rgb]{0,0.2969,0.6016}{\\mathbb{F}_{M}}$ with $\\log_{2}({\\color[rgb]{0,0.2969,0.6016}{M}})\\geq 256$, exceeding native 32-bit word size on GPUs and TPUs. SotA GPU implementations (cite43†Ji et al., 2024 ; cite52†Ma et al., 2023b ) therefore represent each field element as a vector of $D$ 32-bit “digits”.\nL116: A single field multiplication requires two stages: (1) High-precision multiplication, which performs $O(D^{2})$ 32-bit multiplications followed by a $O(D)$ sequential carry propagation of length $D$; and (2) Montgomery reduction, which reduces the $2D$-digit intermediate product back to $D$ digits, incurring another $O(D^{2})$ 32-bit multiplications. We use this SotA radix Montgomery reduction as our arithmetic baseline, termed as radix Mont.\nL117: RNS represents an integer $x$ by residues $(x\\bmod q_{i})$ over coprime moduli $\\{q_{i}\\}$, where modulus ${\\color[rgb]{0.6016,0,0}{Q}}\\!=\\!\\prod_{i}q_{i}$. RNS could lower $O(D^{2})$ down to $O(D)$ when performing modular multiplication with respect to $\\color[rgb]{0.6016,0,0}{Q}$. But the prime modulus $\\color[rgb]{0,0.2969,0.6016}{M}$ cannot be factored into multiple residues, making such reduction inapplicable to ZKP.\nL118: #### 2.3.3. Related Work: Dataflow Optimizations\nL119: Presorting Pippenger (PPG) - MSM In prior work of MSM acceleration on GPU (cite50†Ma et al., 2023a ; cite43†Ji et al., 2024 ), FPGA (cite53†Aasaraai et al., 2022 ; cite54†Liu et al., 2023 ; cite55†Ray et al., 2023 ; cite56†Pottier et al., 2025 ; cite57†Ohno et al., 2025 ) and ASICs (cite44†Zhang et al., 2021 ; cite60†Wang and Gao, 2025 ; cite68†Daftardar et al., 2024 ; cite69†Yang et al., 2025 ; cite70†Liu et al., 2024 ), Presorting PPG is the SotA MSM algorithm.\nL120: Its key idea is to reduce the total number of point additions by first grouping points whose scalars share the same value, summing those points once, and then multiplying the accumulated result by that scalar value. To increase the likelihood of such sharing (“collisions”), each scalar is split into $K=\\left\\lceil{S^{BL}}/{c}\\right\\rceil$ windows of $c$ bits. We adopt presorting PPG as our dataflow baseline, termed as “Presort-PPG”.\nL121: Butterfly NTT and layout invariant 3-step NTT. SotA NTT acceleration on CPUs (cite71†Laine et al., 2018 ; cite72†Buss et al., 2021 ), GPUs (cite51†Dai and Sunar, 2015 ; cite52†Ma et al., 2023b ), FPGAs (cite58†Reagen et al., 2021 ) and ASICs (f1he) implements recursive Cooley-Tukey NTT with minimal computation complexity $O(NlogN)$, commonly called as “butterfly NTT\" in Fig. .\nL122: However, each stage performs fine-grained shuffles that are smaller than vector-register width, leading to costly layout transformations, making TPUs inefficient.\nL123: Recent TPU work (cite48†Tong et al., 2026a ) proposes a layout-invariant 3-step NTT achieving $O(N^{3/2})$ arithmetic with zero layout transformation, breaking the NTT throughput record of the butterfly NTT on GPUs (cite73†Fan et al., 2022 ; cite74†Choi et al., 2025 ) on lower-precision ${\\color[rgb]{0,0.2969,0.6016}{M}}<32\\rm\\ bits$ and lower degree ($N\\leq 2^{17}$).\nL124: It reforms an $N$-input NTT into an $(R,C)$ matrix ($N=R\\times C$) such that an NTT is reformed into matrix and vectorized multiplication for TPU acceleration, as shown in Fig. . We adopt 3-step NTT as dataflow baseline.\nL125: ## 3. Method\nL126: \nL127: This section first defines the Big $T$ notation, then uses it to analyze arithmetic and dataflow inefficiencies of the SotA MSM/NTT algorithm on TPUs, finally showing how MORPH resolves them.\nL128: ### 3.1. Big-$T$ Complexity and TPU’s Parallelism\nL129: \nL130: Today’s parallel computing devices, including GPUs and TPUs, often consist a heterogeneous set of pipelined compute units $\\mathcal{U}=\\{U_{1},U_{2},\\dots,U_{K}\\}$, where each $U_{k}$ denotes a class of units. Let $P_{k}$ be the number of parallel instances of unit type $U_{k}$. We decompose the total work of size $N$ into per-unit contributions $W_{k}(N),k\\in[1,\\dots,K]$, where $W_{k}(N)$ counts the operations mapped to unit $U_{k}$.\nL131: All these on-chip compute units are deeply pipelined, the execution time is bounded by the slowest (bottleneck) pipeline stage. We define the BIG-$T$ complexity (termed as Big-$T$ notation) of an algorithm on a parallel computing device as\nL132: \nL133:  | $$T(N)=O\\Bigl(\\max\\bigl\\{\\max_{k}\\tfrac{W_{k}}{P_{k}},\\;Mem\\bigr\\}\\Bigr)$$  |\nL134: if there exists a constant $c>0$ and $N_{0}$ such that for all $N\\geq N_{0}$, $T(N)\\leq c\\cdot\\max\\Bigl\\{\\max_{k}\\frac{W_{k}}{P_{k}},\\;Mem\\Bigr\\}.$ Here, the term “$\\max_{k}\\frac{W_{k}}{P_{k}}$” captures the bottleneck among heterogeneous pipelined compute units. “$Mem$” captures the overall latency of off-chip data access. Together, they provide an asymptotic lower latency bound of a proposed algorithm on parallel computing devices.\nL135: Under sufficiently large workload, the ideal parallelism of compute units in a TPU used for Big-$T$ analysis is listed in Fig. cite66†2 .\nL136: ### 3.2. Arithmetic Optimizations\nL137: \nL138: Table 1. Big-$T$ Complexity of Arithmetic (Bottleneck in Red)\nL139: Kernel  | VPU Span  | MXU Span  | XLU Span  | Memory Span\nL140: Radix Mont.  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{D^{2}}{PAR_{\\text{VPU}}}$  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{D^{2}}{PAR_{\\text{MXU}}}$  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{D^{2}\\log D}{PAR_{\\text{S}}}$  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{D}{BW_{\\text{HBM}}}$\nL141: MXU RNS Lazy  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{4D}{PAR_{\\text{VPU}}}$  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{D^{2}}{PAR_{\\text{MXU}}}$  | $\\sim$0  | $\\displaystyle\\frac{2D}{BW_{\\text{HBM}}}$\nL142: #### 3.2.1. Challenge: Radix Montgomery Reduction is Dominated by Memory Reorganization\nL143: For a value with $D$ 32-bit digits, radix-$2^{32}$ Montgomery multiplication requires (1) a $O(D^{2})$ big-integer multiplication and (2) a $O(D^{2})$ Montgomery reduction, each containing a sequential $D$-step carry-propagation chain. Carry propagation demands fine-grained shuffling and index permutation on a TPU, which appears as the XLU span bottleneck in Tab. cite75†1 . Thus, the limiting factor is not compute throughput, but the repeated memory reorganization inherent in Radix Montgomery Reduction.\nL144: #### 3.2.2. Solution: Reducing $O(D^{2})\\rightarrow O(2D)$ for Big-Integer Multiplication via RNS\nL145: To eliminate quadratic-cost digit multiplications, we encode each high-precision prime-field element in an extended non-prime field $\\color[rgb]{0.6016,0,0}{\\mathbb{F}_{Q}}$ following (cite64†Jacquemin et al., 2022 ). We select $\\color[rgb]{0.6016,0,0}{Q}>\\color[rgb]{0,0.2969,0.6016}{M}^{2}$ to guarantee that for any $x,y\\in\\color[rgb]{0.6016,0,0}{\\mathbb{F}_{Q}}$, the product of their RNS vectors never overflows $\\color[rgb]{0.6016,0,0}{Q}$, avoiding a true reduction by $\\color[rgb]{0.6016,0,0}{Q}$.\nL146: Hence, the multiplication is fully decomposed into independent 32-bit limb multiplications. This transformation converts the compute pattern from quadratic all-to-all digit multiplications into linear digit-independent multiplications.\nL147: #### 3.2.3. Solution: Reducing $O(D^{2})$ Reduction via MXU-Centric RNS Lazy Reduction\nL148: Although multiplication is linearized in $\\color[rgb]{0.6016,0,0}{\\mathbb{F}_{Q}}$, modular reduction by $\\color[rgb]{0,0.2969,0.6016}{M}$ must still be performed over the prime field $\\color[rgb]{0,0.2969,0.6016}{\\mathbb{F}_{M}}$. A naïve approach would require RNS reconstruction from $\\color[rgb]{0.6016,0,0}{\\mathbb{F}_{Q}}$ $\\rightarrow$ prime-field reduction ($\\bmod\\ {\\color[rgb]{0,0.2969,0.6016}{M}})$ $\\rightarrow$ RNS decomposition into $\\color[rgb]{0.6016,0,0}{\\mathbb{F}_{Q}}$.\nL149: We reduce this overhead by applying Basis Aligned Transformation (cite48†Tong et al., 2026a ) to Simon’s reduction (cite65†Langowski and Devadas, 2025 ), collapsing this multi-stage pipeline into one 8-bit matrix multiplication (Lines 15 and 18 in Alg.cite76†1 ) plus four 32-bit vector operations (Lines 16,17,19,20 in Alg.cite76†1 ). This shifts the dominant $O(D^{2})$ term to a low-precision matrix multiplication, with its cost amortized by MXU, yielding $O(D)$ latency overhead.\nL150: It also removes all carry-propagation from the critical path, making it throughput-bound rather than shuffle-bound.\nL151: #### 3.2.4. Walk-Through Example\nL152: \nL153: Figure 3. Illustration of actual value change and computational flow of MXU-centric RNS Lazy Modular Multiplication.\nL154: Given $x,y\\in\\color[rgb]{0,0.2969,0.6016}{\\mathbb{F}_{M}}$, we convert them into extended-field RNS form $x_{\\color[rgb]{0.6016,0,0}{Q}},y_{\\color[rgb]{0.6016,0,0}{Q}}$, each containing $2D$ 32-bit digits. The modular multiplication proceeds as:\nL155: $\\bullet$ RNS-level 32-bit VecMul with Montgomery reduction, $O(2D)$.\nL156: $\\bullet$ MXU-centric RNS Lazy Reduction (Lines 14–20 of Alg.cite76†1 , $O(D^{2})$).\nL157: All sequential carry propagation are removed. Computation becomes dominated by vectorized operations, as highlighted in Tab. cite75†1 .\nL158: In summary, MORPH encapsulates computation over $\\color[rgb]{0,0.2969,0.6016}{\\mathbb{F}_{M}}$ inside a larger field $\\color[rgb]{0.6016,0,0}{\\mathbb{F}_{Q}}$, paying (1) a 2$\\times$ memory footprint for the extended RNS representation, and (2) additional RNS reconstruction/decomposition.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMORPH/configurations.toml at main · EfficientPPML/MORPH · GitHub (https://github.com/EfficientPPML/MORPH/blob/main/configurations.toml)\nciteturn5view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/EfficientPPML/MORPH/blob/main/configurations.toml\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 305\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBump rust crates' version · ingonyama-zk/icicle@6c77775 · GitHub (https://github.com/ingonyama-zk/icicle/commit/6c77775961b0e4dc2be359feec1ac11536c18043)\nciteturn5v